-- Every skill a dragon can bring to a fight: three per species
-- (DragonDefs Skills), 108 in all, none of them shared.
--
-- A skill is data only, and it is deliberately thin: who it hits, how hard,
-- how often, and which STATUSES it puts on (Config/StatusDefs). It never
-- carries its own numbers for a burn, a buff or a shield - those live on the
-- status, the same for every skill that applies it. That is what lets the
-- battle screen show an icon instead of a line of text, and it makes damage
-- over time depend on the VICTIM's Health rather than the attacker's Attack.
--
-- ELEMENTS (Config/ElementChart). Every skill has exactly one, and a name
-- belongs to one element for good. A species only ever carries skills of its
-- own elements: a Common's three are all its element, a hybrid brings two of
-- one and one of the other. The element decides how hard the skill lands:
-- more against the element it beats, half against its own.
--
-- NO TWO SKILLS DO THE SAME JOB: same target, same statuses and same
-- cooldown is the same skill, whatever damage each one does - there would be
-- no reason to bring the weaker one. SkillRules.Validate refuses it.
--
-- Fields:
--   DisplayName  shown in the UI.
--   Element      Fire | Water | Nature | Earth | Electric | Metal | Dark | Light
--   Class        the class it was written for (Config/DragonClasses). Giving a
--                dragon one skill off its own class is deliberate.
--   Target       "Enemy" | "AllEnemies" | "Self" | "Ally" | "AllAllies" |
--                "WeakestAlly" (the ally with the least Health left).
--   Power        damage, as a share of the caster's Attack (nil = no damage).
--   Cooldown     turns to wait before using it again (0 = every turn).
--   Effects      statuses put on whoever the skill lands on.
--   SelfEffects  statuses the caster puts on itself, whoever it hits.
-- SkillRules.Describe turns all of this into the line the UI shows, so the
-- numbers and the text can never drift apart.
